After some debugging, I inspected the pprof with flame graph when delete a pod, it shows that `subpath.doCleanSubPaths` and `filepath.Walk` cost much more time, here is the graph:

![2](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/16742217/150320838-637e648c-ca95-4d8b-9114-d9e217faa069.PNG)

Apparently the Walk func spent much time when doCleanPath, what we care is the top level dir,and this pr [#82698](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/82698) has added some fast path to avoid much recursive time, but before gol1.16, the filepath.Walk is still less efficient, because when used in kubelet, it reads all the dir in the mount point before call the callback func in Walk even we don't use it after that, so it spends much time.

And after go 1.16, filepath.WalkDir is introduced, which is more efficient than Walk, as the comment said in golang https://github.com/golang/go/blob/master/src/io/fs/walk.go#L54
```
// The differences between WalkDirFunc compared to filepath.WalkFunc are:
//
//   - The second argument has type fs.DirEntry instead of fs.FileInfo.
//   - The function is called before reading a directory, to allow SkipDir
//     to bypass the directory read entirely.
//   - If a directory read fails, the function is called a second time
//     for that directory to report the error.
//
```

WalkDirFunc is called before reading a directory, so it can save much time when there are lots of dirs in the first level of mount points. So it's time wasting and meaningless when we use Walk so far because we traverse the dir but we didn't use it and return SkipDir err, the code is [here](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/master/pkg/volume/util/subpath/subpath_linux.go#L275)

After I replaced it with `filepath.WalkDir`, the `doCleanSubPaths` took only about 41ms, which is a huge boost than 17s before.
